<p>
In a nice coda to yesterday&rsquo;s post <a href="https://irreal.org/blog/?p=14131">on Sacha Chua&rsquo;s solution for entering number ranges</a>, <u>0-_</u>-0_ points out that <a href="https://www.reddit.com/r/emacs/comments/1wywgif/tip_generating_number_ranges/">a solution already exists</a>. That solution is abo-abo&rsquo;s <a href="https://github.com/abo-abo/tiny">tiny</a> library. I vaguely remember seeing tiny when abo-abo released it 13 years ago but I never followed up and used it. Still, anything from abo-abo is worth taking a look at and, in any event, we now have two solutions to the problem.
</p>
<p>
Abo-abo&rsquo;s solution brings to mind another Emacs lesson: very often, if you need some functionality, there&rsquo;s a good chance that it already exists. Of course, that&rsquo;s not the same as being easily discoverable. An internal function can usually be discovered through the on-line documentation but a third party library like tiny won&rsquo;t appear in that documentation until it&rsquo;s loaded.
</p>
